<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.2.4: Making a Perfect Square</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02357</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.2.4: Making a Perfect Square</md:title>
    <md:uuid>c2834b44-2284-4795-b01a-7dce24c66b81</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>For questions 1 – 3, what could be added to each expression to make it a perfect square?</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Cool Down Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="543b9b67-dd3b-4a76-ade2-553feaddce4c" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter what could be added to make the expression a perfect square.</para><para>\(x^2+12x\)</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The constant term \(c=36\) can be added to make the perfect square \(x^2+12x+36\).</para><para>The correct answer is: The constant term \(c=36\) can be added to make the perfect square \(x^2+12x+36\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="b90d8ac3-5961-4630-8cd0-4f4dca82c72c" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter what could be added to make the expression a perfect square.</para><para>\(x^2−6x+1\)</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 8 can be added to the constant term \(c=1\) to make the perfect square \(x^2-6x+9\).</para><para>The correct answer is: 8 can be added to the constant term \(c=1\) to make the perfect square \(x^2-6x+9\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="5eeca6f7-4863-4592-ae57-aafccf5fa58c" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter what could be added to make the expression a perfect square.</para><para>\(x^2+14x−10\)</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 59 can be added to the constant term \(c=-10\) to make the perfect square \(x^2+14x+49\).</para><para>The correct answer is: 59 can be added to the constant term \(c=-10\) to make the perfect square \(x^2+14x+49\).</para></solution></exercise></section><example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Solve the equation \(x^2−16x=-60\) by <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">completing the square</term>. Show your reasoning.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(\begin{aligned} x^{2}-16 x &amp;=-60 \\ x^{2}-16 x+64 &amp;=-60+64 \\ x^{2}-16 x+64 &amp;=4 \\ (x-8)^{2} &amp;=4 \\ \sqrt{(x-8)^{2}} &amp;=\sqrt{4} \\ x-8 &amp;=\pm 2 \\ x-8 &amp;=2 \\ x-8 &amp;=-2 \\ \\ x=6 &amp; \text { or } x=10 \end{aligned}\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
